 "sq": [
  {
   "_id": 5162,
   "ANSWER": "<p style=\"text-align:justify; margin-bottom:11px\"><span style=\"font-size:11pt\"><span style=\"line-height:107%\"><span style=\"font-family:Aptos,sans-serif\">&nbsp;A stack is a Last In, First Out (LIFO) data structure that can be implemented using an array or a linked list. When implemented using a linked list, each element (node) contains two parts: data and a pointer to the next node.</span></span></span></p>\n\n<p style=\"margin-bottom:11px\"><span style=\"font-size:11pt\"><span style=\"line-height:107%\"><span style=\"font-family:Aptos,sans-serif\"><b>Advantages:</b></span></span></span></p>\n\n<ol>\n\t<li style=\"text-align:justify; margin-bottom:11px\"><span style=\"font-size:11pt\"><span style=\"tab-stops:list 36.0pt\"><span style=\"line-height:107%\"><span style=\"font-family:Aptos,sans-serif\">Dynamic Size</span></span></span></span>\n\n\t<ul style=\"list-style-type:circle\">\n\t\t<li style=\"text-align:justify; margin-bottom:11px\"><span style=\"font-size:11pt\"><span style=\"tab-stops:list 72.0pt\"><span style=\"line-height:107%\"><span style=\"font-family:Aptos,sans-serif\">Unlike arrays, a linked list does not require a pre-defined size.</span></span></span></span></li>\n\t\t<li style=\"text-align:justify; margin-bottom:11px\"><span style=\"font-size:11pt\"><span style=\"tab-stops:list 72.0pt\"><span style=\"line-height:107%\"><span style=\"font-family:Aptos,sans-serif\">Memory is allocated dynamically, so it can grow or shrink as needed.</span></span></span></span></li>\n\t</ul>\n\t</li>\n\t<li style=\"text-align:justify; margin-bottom:11px\"><span style=\"font-size:11pt\"><span style=\"tab-stops:list 36.0pt\"><span style=\"line-height:107%\"><span style=\"font-family:Aptos,sans-serif\">Efficient Insertions &amp; Deletions</span></span></span></span>\n\t<ul style=\"list-style-type:circle\">\n\t\t<li style=\"text-align:justify; margin-bottom:11px\"><span style=\"font-size:11pt\"><span style=\"tab-stops:list 72.0pt\"><span style=\"line-height:107%\"><span style=\"font-family:Aptos,sans-serif\">Operations like push (insertion) and pop (deletion) are performed in constant time O(1)O(1)O(1) at the head of the list.</span></span></span></span></li>\n\t\t<li style=\"text-align:justify; margin-bottom:11px\"><span style=\"font-size:11pt\"><span style=\"tab-stops:list 72.0pt\"><span style=\"line-height:107%\"><span style=\"font-family:Aptos,sans-serif\">No shifting of elements is required, unlike in arrays.</span></span></span></span></li>\n\t</ul>\n\t</li>\n\t<li style=\"text-align:justify; margin-bottom:11px\"><span style=\"font-size:11pt\"><span style=\"tab-stops:list 36.0pt\"><span style=\"line-height:107%\"><span style=\"font-family:Aptos,sans-serif\">No Wastage of Memory</span></span></span></span>\n\t<ul style=\"list-style-type:circle\">\n\t\t<li style=\"text-align:justify; margin-bottom:11px\"><span style=\"font-size:11pt\"><span style=\"tab-stops:list 72.0pt\"><span style=\"line-height:107%\"><span style=\"font-family:Aptos,sans-serif\">In an array-based stack, if the allocated size is too large, memory is wasted.</span></span></span></span></li>\n\t\t<li style=\"text-align:justify; margin-bottom:11px\"><span style=\"font-size:11pt\"><span style=\"tab-stops:list 72.0pt\"><span style=\"line-height:107%\"><span style=\"font-family:Aptos,sans-serif\">In a linked list, memory is allocated only for the required number of elements, avoiding unnecessary memory usage.</span></span></span></span></li>\n\t</ul>\n\t</li>\n</ol>\n\n<p style=\"margin-bottom:11px\"><span style=\"font-size:11pt\"><span style=\"line-height:107%\"><span style=\"font-family:Aptos,sans-serif\"><b>Disadvantages:</b></span></span></span></p>\n\n<ol>\n\t<li style=\"text-align:justify; margin-bottom:11px\"><span style=\"font-size:11pt\"><span style=\"tab-stops:list 36.0pt\"><span style=\"line-height:107%\"><span style=\"font-family:Aptos,sans-serif\">Extra Memory Overhead</span></span></span></span>\n\n\t<ul style=\"list-style-type:circle\">\n\t\t<li style=\"text-align:justify; margin-bottom:11px\"><span style=\"font-size:11pt\"><span style=\"tab-stops:list 72.0pt\"><span style=\"line-height:107%\"><span style=\"font-family:Aptos,sans-serif\">Each node in the linked list requires additional memory for the pointer.</span></span></span></span></li>\n\t\t<li style=\"text-align:justify; margin-bottom:11px\"><span style=\"font-size:11pt\"><span style=\"tab-stops:list 72.0pt\"><span style=\"line-height:107%\"><span style=\"font-family:Aptos,sans-serif\">This increases memory consumption compared to arrays.</span></span></span></span></li>\n\t</ul>\n\t</li>\n\t<li style=\"text-align:justify; margin-bottom:11px\"><span style=\"font-size:11pt\"><span style=\"tab-stops:list 36.0pt\"><span style=\"line-height:107%\"><span style=\"font-family:Aptos,sans-serif\">Cache Inefficiency</span></span></span></span>\n\t<ul style=\"list-style-type:circle\">\n\t\t<li style=\"text-align:justify; margin-bottom:11px\"><span style=\"font-size:11pt\"><span style=\"tab-stops:list 72.0pt\"><span style=\"line-height:107%\"><span style=\"font-family:Aptos,sans-serif\">Arrays are stored in contiguous memory locations, making them more cache-friendly.</span></span></span></span></li>\n\t\t<li style=\"text-align:justify; margin-bottom:11px\"><span style=\"font-size:11pt\"><span style=\"tab-stops:list 72.0pt\"><span style=\"line-height:107%\"><span style=\"font-family:Aptos,sans-serif\">Linked lists are stored in scattered memory locations, leading to slower access due to cache misses.</span></span></span></span></li>\n\t</ul>\n\t</li>\n\t<li style=\"text-align:justify; margin-bottom:11px\"><span style=\"font-size:11pt\"><span style=\"tab-stops:list 36.0pt\"><span style=\"line-height:107%\"><span style=\"font-family:Aptos,sans-serif\">Complex Implementation</span></span></span></span>\n\t<ul style=\"list-style-type:circle\">\n\t\t<li style=\"text-align:justify; margin-bottom:11px\"><span style=\"font-size:11pt\"><span style=\"tab-stops:list 72.0pt\"><span style=\"line-height:107%\"><span style=\"font-family:Aptos,sans-serif\">Implementing a linked list requires managing pointers properly.</span></span></span></span></li>\n\t\t<li style=\"text-align:justify; margin-bottom:11px\"><span style=\"font-size:11pt\"><span style=\"tab-stops:list 72.0pt\"><span style=\"line-height:107%\"><span style=\"font-family:Aptos,sans-serif\">If pointers are not handled correctly, memory leaks or segmentation faults may occur<b>.</b></span></span></span></span></li>\n\t</ul>\n\t</li>\n</ol>\n\n<p style=\"margin-bottom:11px\">&nbsp;</p>\n",
   "COURSE_CODE": "21CSC201J",
   "LEVEL": 2,
   "OUTCOMES": [
    "PLO1",
    "PLO2",
    "PLO3",
    "PLO12",
    "PLO13"
   ],
   "QUESTION_DESC": "<p style=\"margin-bottom:11px\"><span style=\"font-size:11pt\"><span style=\"line-height:107%\"><span style=\"font-family:Aptos,sans-serif\"><b>Discuss the advantages and disadvantages of implementing a stack using a linked list.</b></span></span></span></p>\n",
   "SESSION_ID": 303,
   "TYPE": "sq",
   "key": 5162
  },
  {
   "_id": 5163,
   "ANSWER": "<p style=\"margin-bottom:11px\"><span style=\"font-size:11pt\"><span style=\"line-height:107%\"><span style=\"font-family:Aptos,sans-serif\"><b>1. Undo/Redo Operations in Text Editors</b></span></span></span></p>\n\n<ul>\n\t<li style=\"text-align:justify; margin-bottom:11px\"><span style=\"font-size:11pt\"><span style=\"tab-stops:list 36.0pt\"><span style=\"line-height:107%\"><span style=\"font-family:Aptos,sans-serif\">Most text editors (like Microsoft Word, Notepad, and Photoshop) use stacks for undo/redo functionality.</span></span></span></span></li>\n\t<li style=\"text-align:justify; margin-bottom:11px\"><span style=\"font-size:11pt\"><span style=\"tab-stops:list 36.0pt\"><span style=\"line-height:107%\"><span style=\"font-family:Aptos,sans-serif\">Every action performed (like typing a word or deleting text) is pushed onto the stack.</span></span></span></span></li>\n\t<li style=\"text-align:justify; margin-bottom:11px\"><span style=\"font-size:11pt\"><span style=\"tab-stops:list 36.0pt\"><span style=\"line-height:107%\"><span style=\"font-family:Aptos,sans-serif\">When &quot;Undo&quot; is clicked, the last action is popped from the stack, reversing the change.</span></span></span></span></li>\n\t<li style=\"text-align:justify; margin-bottom:11px\"><span style=\"font-size:11pt\"><span style=\"tab-stops:list 36.0pt\"><span style=\"line-height:107%\"><span style=\"font-family:Aptos,sans-serif\">If &quot;Redo&quot; is clicked, the undone action is pushed back onto the stack.</span></span></span></span></li>\n\t<li style=\"text-align:justify; margin-bottom:11px\"><span style=\"font-size:11pt\"><span style=\"tab-stops:list 36.0pt\"><span style=\"line-height:107%\"><span style=\"font-family:Aptos,sans-serif\">Example:</span></span></span></span>\n\t<ul style=\"list-style-type:circle\">\n\t\t<li style=\"text-align:justify; margin-bottom:11px\"><span style=\"font-size:11pt\"><span style=\"tab-stops:list 72.0pt\"><span style=\"line-height:107%\"><span style=\"font-family:Aptos,sans-serif\">Typing &quot;Hello&quot; in MS Word &rarr; Each keystroke is pushed onto the stack.</span></span></span></span></li>\n\t\t<li style=\"text-align:justify; margin-bottom:11px\"><span style=\"font-size:11pt\"><span style=\"tab-stops:list 72.0pt\"><span style=\"line-height:107%\"><span style=\"font-family:Aptos,sans-serif\">Pressing Ctrl+Z (Undo) &rarr; The last action (typing &#39;o&#39;) is popped, and &quot;Hell&quot; remains.</span></span></span></span></li>\n\t\t<li style=\"text-align:justify; margin-bottom:11px\"><span style=\"font-size:11pt\"><span style=\"tab-stops:list 72.0pt\"><span style=\"line-height:107%\"><span style=\"font-family:Aptos,sans-serif\">Pressing Ctrl+Y (Redo) &rarr; The popped action is restored, bringing back &quot;Hello&quot;.</span></span></span></span></li>\n\t</ul>\n\t</li>\n</ul>\n\n<p style=\"margin-bottom:11px\"><span style=\"font-size:11pt\"><span style=\"line-height:107%\"><span style=\"font-family:Aptos,sans-serif\"><b>2. Browser Back and Forward Navigation</b></span></span></span></p>\n\n<ul>\n\t<li style=\"text-align:justify; margin-bottom:11px\"><span style=\"font-size:11pt\"><span style=\"tab-stops:list 36.0pt\"><span style=\"line-height:107%\"><span style=\"font-family:Aptos,sans-serif\">Web browsers use two stacks to manage the &quot;Back&quot; and &quot;Forward&quot; navigation.</span></span></span></span></li>\n\t<li style=\"text-align:justify; margin-bottom:11px\"><span style=\"font-size:11pt\"><span style=\"tab-stops:list 36.0pt\"><span style=\"line-height:107%\"><span style=\"font-family:Aptos,sans-serif\">When you visit a new webpage, it is pushed onto the back stack.</span></span></span></span></li>\n\t<li style=\"text-align:justify; margin-bottom:11px\"><span style=\"font-size:11pt\"><span style=\"tab-stops:list 36.0pt\"><span style=\"line-height:107%\"><span style=\"font-family:Aptos,sans-serif\">Clicking the &quot;Back&quot; button pops the top page from the stack and loads the previous page.</span></span></span></span></li>\n\t<li style=\"text-align:justify; margin-bottom:11px\"><span style=\"font-size:11pt\"><span style=\"tab-stops:list 36.0pt\"><span style=\"line-height:107%\"><span style=\"font-family:Aptos,sans-serif\">If you click &quot;Forward,&quot; the page is pushed onto another stack, allowing you to revisit it.</span></span></span></span></li>\n</ul>\n\n<p style=\"text-align:justify; margin-bottom:11px\"><span style=\"font-size:11pt\"><span style=\"line-height:107%\"><span style=\"font-family:Aptos,sans-serif\">Example:</span></span></span></p>\n\n<ol>\n\t<li style=\"text-align:justify; margin-bottom:11px\"><span style=\"font-size:11pt\"><span style=\"tab-stops:list 36.0pt\"><span style=\"line-height:107%\"><span style=\"font-family:Aptos,sans-serif\">Open Google.com &rarr; Pushed onto the Back Stack</span></span></span></span></li>\n\t<li style=\"text-align:justify; margin-bottom:11px\"><span style=\"font-size:11pt\"><span style=\"tab-stops:list 36.0pt\"><span style=\"line-height:107%\"><span style=\"font-family:Aptos,sans-serif\">Open Facebook.com &rarr; Pushed onto the Back Stack</span></span></span></span></li>\n\t<li style=\"text-align:justify; margin-bottom:11px\"><span style=\"font-size:11pt\"><span style=\"tab-stops:list 36.0pt\"><span style=\"line-height:107%\"><span style=\"font-family:Aptos,sans-serif\">Click &quot;Back&quot; &rarr; Facebook is popped, and Google is displayed.</span></span></span></span></li>\n\t<li style=\"text-align:justify; margin-bottom:11px\"><span style=\"font-size:11pt\"><span style=\"tab-stops:list 36.0pt\"><span style=\"line-height:107%\"><span style=\"font-family:Aptos,sans-serif\">Click &quot;Forward&quot; &rarr; Facebook is pushed again, and it is displayed.</span></span></span></span></li>\n</ol>\n\n<p style=\"margin-bottom:11px\">&nbsp;</p>\n",
